WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.964
Ordinary pseudo-random generators are useful engineering tools.

2
00:00:02.964 --> 00:00:08.521
They support simulations, games, randomized tests, sampling, load balancing, scheduling experiments, and reproducible bug investigations.

3
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:11.484
In those contexts, determinism can be a feature.

4
00:00:11.484 --> 00:00:18.894
If a test fails with a particular seed, the team can run the same seed again and reproduce the sequence.

5
00:00:18.894 --> 00:00:24.451
If a simulation needs a controlled comparison, a known generator state helps make results repeatable.

6
00:00:24.451 --> 00:00:26.674
Security randomness has a different goal.

7
00:00:26.674 --> 00:00:35.194
A security value should be difficult to predict, even when an observer knows the algorithm family, understands the application, and has seen other

8
00:00:35.194 --> 00:00:35.935
generated values.

9
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:42.974
Repeatability, which is helpful in a test, is dangerous when the value protects a token, key, challenge, or session.

10
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:51.865
A generator that is excellent for simulation can still be the wrong tool for a security boundary because it was never designed to withstand

11
00:00:51.865 --> 00:00:52.236
prediction.

12
00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:58.163
A practical question helps keep the decision grounded: does this value need fairness, repeatability, or unpredictability?

13
00:00:58.163 --> 00:01:00.386
Fairness may point to a distribution.

14
00:01:00.386 --> 00:01:03.350
Repeatability may point to a deterministic test source.

15
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:09.648
Unpredictability points to a cryptographically secure source such as a platform CSPRNG or an approved cryptographic library.

16
00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:12.241
API names can be misleading without context.

17
00:01:12.241 --> 00:01:19.280
The safe approach is to label the use case first, then select the random source that matches the requirement.
